  "metadata": {
    "description": "Two-instance coexistence driver: ONE grid+rule family (grids/cartesian_uniform_1d/rules/central_D2_zero_grad_bc, which transitively pulls the interior stencil and the grid) imported TWICE into one model under distinct namespace prefixes (esm-spec 9.7.7) — the composition that was inexpressible before import-renaming + match-scoping landed. Edge A: prefix meshA, N closed to 8 at the edge, free spacing name dx rebound to meshA_dx. Edge B: prefix meshB, N closed to 16, dx rebound to meshB_dx. Renaming is transitive and simultaneous: the index set x arrives as meshA.x / meshB.x, the rule instance's compound match wrt follows it (D(D(f, meshA.x), meshA.x)), the metaparameter N was folded per edge into the makearray regions and ranges, and — the point of this case — each instance's `where: {f: {shape: [x]}}` shape constraint (esm-spec 9.6.1) is rewritten to [meshA.x] / [meshB.x], so instance A is eligible ONLY on second derivatives of fields declared over meshA.x and instance B only over meshB.x. The two 8- and 16-cell heat fields evolve completely independently, each lowered by its own mesh's rule with its own spacing, with no first-declared-wins collision and no priority arbitration. The free-name rebind of dx is the same mechanism the MPAS keyed-factor family uses to point two meshes at their own arrays; here it lets each grid instance carry its own physical spacing (1/8 vs 1/16). MMS: each field solves the 1-D heat equation u_t = alpha u_xx on the unit interval [0,1] with zero-gradient (homogeneous Neumann) boundaries and manufactured solution u(x,t) = exp(-alpha pi^2 t) cos(pi x) (alpha = 0.01): cos(pi x) has u_x = 0 at x=0 and x=1, so it is compatible with the zero-gradient rule and needs no forcing, and u_xx = -pi^2 u gives u_t = alpha u_xx exactly (the same manufactured solution as problems/heat_1d_zero_grad.esm, run here at two resolutions in one document). Initial condition and test references spell the grid's index-to-coordinate conversion x_i = (i - 1/2) dx inline with load-foldable scalars ((i - 0.5)*0.125 and (i - 0.5)*0.0625), never invoking the renamed x_coord templates: build-time cellwise evaluation is scope-free (model parameters/observeds out of scope), so build-time coordinate expressions fold from literals alone. The spacing factor inside each coordinate aggregate is spelled as the exact non-integral decimal 1/N (0.125 = 1/8, 0.0625 = 1/16) rather than the integer ratio {op:/,args:[1,N]}: Julia's aggregate lowering promotes integer `/` operands to Float64 (1 -> 1.0) while the finite-difference and other bindings preserve the integer literals, so an integer ratio inside an aggregate expr would not serialize byte-identically across the five bindings, whereas a non-integral decimal is carried verbatim by every binding (the ast conformance requirement). The physical-spacing observeds meshA_dx/meshB_dx keep the integer-ratio 1/N spelling because they sit outside any aggregate (the rule body's free dx resolves to them) and render identically as integers. The zero-gradient D2 stencil telescopes, so the discrete mean of each field is conserved exactly (and equals 0, since cos(pi x) is antisymmetric about the cell-center-symmetric x=1/2 for both even cell counts) — the mean assertions pin that conservation independently of the truncation-bounded L2 assertions.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "name": "two_cartesian_grids_coexist",
    "references": [
